<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
            http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <!--
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      007 — THE MULTI-BRAND CUT-OVER RE-STAMP (issue #19)

      One-shot data migration that carries the rows of a SINGLE-ISSUER wiki-bff across
      to the brand name it is about to be given, at the moment the operator turns on
      `APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS`. It is INERT until armed (see "Arming" below), so a
      single-tenant cluster is untouched.

      ── WHY IT IS NEEDED (measured, not assumed) ─────────────────────────────────
      Issue #19's premise — "rows written before brand scoping carry domain='default'"
      — does NOT hold for this repo, and the correction is in the CODE, not in git
      archaeology (the history is a squashed 2026-07-29 snapshot and cannot testify
      about anything before it). The direct evidence:

        * all four domain-bearing tables declare `domain varchar(64)` with
          `defaultValue="default"` AND `<constraints nullable="false"/>` (001..004), so
          no row has ever been able to exist without a domain; and
        * `PrincipalDomain.forScope` derives the domain SERVER-SIDE from the JWT issuer
          on every write path, so no client has ever been able to choose one.

      There is therefore no "pre-brand-scoping epoch" population to repair. What DOES
      exist is a cut-over hazard, documented in the platform manifest (hugmesh-generator
      `specs/cluster/manifests/wiki-bff-api/deployment.yaml`, the "APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS is
      deliberately NOT set here" comment):

        * UNSET `APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS` ⇒ commons' DomainRegistry seeds the back-compat
          single-issuer entry {CASDOOR_ISSUER_URI -> "default"}, so `domainOf()` is
          "default" for every principal — which is exactly what every existing row is
          stamped with. Everything matches.
        * SET it to "https://id.<domain>=<brand>" ⇒ `domainOf()` starts returning
          "<brand>", so every `WHERE domain = ?` read stops matching the existing
          "default" rows. A SINGLE pair also leaves `isMultiIssuer()` false, so the
          fail-closed multi-issuer decoder never activates: no 401, no error — the
          pages, the attachments, the audit trail and (worst) the `wiki_access`
          GRANTS AND BANS simply vanish. Later writes stamp NEW rows "<brand>", so an
          un-migrated rollback leaves split-brain data.

      A silently-dropped BAN is the sharp edge here: `WikiAccessGuard` reads
      `wiki_access` scoped by domain, so a banned principal whose ban row is stranded
      at "default" is silently un-banned the moment the brand is configured.

      ── WHICH BRAND, AND WHY THAT ONE ────────────────────────────────────────────
      Issue #19 asked for "their real brand, or a documented sentinel if the real brand
      is unrecoverable". The real brand IS recoverable — but not from this database.
      Nothing in the wiki schema records an intended brand for a "default" row: the
      page, its attachments, its audit rows and its ACL rows are ALL stamped with the
      same coerced "default", and there is no subject→brand mapping anywhere in it. The
      brand is recoverable because before the cut-over the deployment has exactly ONE
      brand, and the operator is in the act of naming it. So the target brand is an
      OPERATOR-SUPPLIED changelog parameter, not a guess — and this migration is run
      as part of the cut-over, not speculatively.

      NOT a sentinel, deliberately — but the discriminator is NARROWER than it looks,
      so state it exactly. Re-stamping to a synthetic sentinel (`__unbranded__` and
      friends) changes no read reachability (no principal resolves either "default" or a
      sentinel once a brand is configured). The real difference is that a BRAND is
      resolvable by that brand's own scoped principals, who therefore get their pages,
      attachments, audit trail and ACL overlay back; a sentinel is resolvable by nobody
      and the rows are lost to everyone. On the SUPERADMIN axis the two options cost
      exactly the same — see the next section, which is the honest version of a claim an
      earlier draft of this header got wrong by presenting the SUPERADMIN breakage as
      unique to the sentinel.

      ── COST THIS MIGRATION ITSELF INCURS: SUPERADMIN WRITES TO MIGRATED ROWS ────
      Disclosed, not hidden, and MEASURED (`DefaultDomainRestampChangesetTest`
      → `SuperadminCostOfMigrating`): after the re-stamp, a SUPERADMIN can no longer
      WRITE the rows it moved.

      `PrincipalDomain.forScope` returns null for a SUPERADMIN (the cross-domain read
      bypass), and the REMAINING WRITE paths coerce that null to
      `DomainRegistry.DEFAULT_DOMAIN` — WikiPageRepository:104 (create), :132
      (updateBySlug), :151 (publishBySlug), WikiAttachmentRepository:93/117,
      WikiAuditLogRepository:60, WikiAccessRepository:70. So such a SUPERADMIN write is
      literally `... WHERE domain = 'default'`, and after this migration there are no
      'default' rows left for it to match. Concretely, on a migrated page a SUPERADMIN's
      update answers a nonsense 409 and their publish a 404.

      DELETE NO LONGER BELONGS ON THAT LIST (issue #15, fixed). `WikiService.delete`
      now resolves the page in the caller's scope and deletes BY THE RESOLVED ROW'S ID
      (WikiPageRepository:170 `deleteById` — no domain predicate at all), so a
      SUPERADMIN delete reaches a migrated row correctly, a delete that matches nothing
      is a 404 rather than a 204, and it can never remove `default`'s same-slug page.
      Attachments go with the page in the same statement via the `wiki_attachment` FK
      ON DELETE CASCADE (002), so nothing is destroyed ahead of a delete that fails.

      This is the SAME cost the rejected sentinel would have carried; it is not a reason
      to prefer either. The brand's own scoped ADMIN/EDITOR principals DO regain full
      write access to every migrated row, which is what makes the brand the right target.
      The residual SUPERADMIN gap is now create/update/publish only (and the attachment,
      audit and access writes above): those must use the null cross-domain scope instead
      of coercing to DEFAULT_DOMAIN before a SUPERADMIN can fully administer a
      post-cut-over deployment. That caveat applies to the MIGRATED rows just as much as
      to the rows this migration deliberately leaves alone.

      ── WHAT THIS TOUCHES (exhaustive) ──────────────────────────────────────────
      Only the `domain` column, only where it equals the literal 'default', in the four
      tables that carry one:

        wiki_page.domain        (001)  — the pages themselves
        wiki_attachment.domain  (002)  — the denormalised copy of the owning page's
                                         domain; attachment reads scope on THIS column
                                         (`WikiAttachmentDataRepository`: the JOIN
                                         predicate is `a.domain = :domain`, not
                                         `p.domain`), so leaving it behind would strand
                                         every attachment of a migrated page
        wiki_audit_log.domain   (003)  — the content-edit trail. Included because these
                                         rows are not being FALSIFIED: they always
                                         belonged to this one brand, whose NAME is what
                                         is changing. Stranding them hides the brand's
                                         own history from its own admins.
        wiki_access.domain      (004)  — the grant / timeout / BAN overlay. The
                                         security-critical one (see above).

      No row is created, deleted, or altered in any other column. No DDL. Ids, slugs,
      versions, content, timestamps and `state` are untouched.

      NOT touched: rows whose domain is anything other than 'default' (they already name
      a real brand, or the `__deny__` fail-closed sentinel, and are left exactly as they
      are), and every other table/column in the schema.

      ── PROVING THE SELECTION ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
      The predicate is `WHERE domain = 'default'` — a literal equality on a NOT NULL
      column, so it is total and unambiguous. Its correctness rests on ONE claim: that
      on the deployment being cut over, EVERY 'default' row belongs to the single brand
      being named. That holds because with `APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS` unset there is exactly
      one issuer and `domainOf()` returns "default" for every principal, so 'default' is
      that brand's only possible stamp.

      This is asserted, not assumed, by `DefaultDomainRestampChangesetTest`, which drives
      THIS changelog over a real Postgres: un-armed leaves every row at 'default' and the
      changeset PENDING; armed moves every 'default' row in all four tables to the target
      and leaves other brands' rows byte-identical; the rollback returns them.

      Known limitation, stated rather than papered over: on an ALREADY multi-brand
      cluster, 'default' rows can also be produced by a SUPERADMIN write (their scope is
      null and `WikiPageRepository` coerces it to DEFAULT_DOMAIN). For THAT population
      the intended brand really is unrecoverable, and this migration is not the tool —
      running it there would sweep SUPERADMIN-authored rows into whichever brand was
      named. Arm this only on a cluster that is single-issuer TODAY.

      ── THE FORWARD RUN CAN COLLIDE, AND THAT IS A BOOT FAILURE ─────────────────
      If a row already at `<brand>` shares a unique key with a 'default' row being moved
      onto it, the UPDATE violates `uq_wiki_page_domain_slug` (same slug at both stamps)
      or `uq_wiki_access_active` (same subject+access_type active at both). The run then
      throws `liquibase.exception.DatabaseException: ERROR: duplicate key value violates
      unique constraint ...`.

      Atomicity holds — proven by `DefaultDomainRestampChangesetTest`
      → `ForwardCollisionFailsLoudlyAndAtomically`: the transaction rolls back, every row
      keeps its pre-migration stamp, and the changeset is still absent from
      `databasechangelog`, so nothing is half-migrated and the migration can be re-run
      once the collision is resolved. But because Liquibase runs at application startup,
      the operator sees this as THE APPLICATION FAILING TO BOOT, not as a migration
      warning. Budget for that in the maintenance window.

      The precondition for a collision is precisely the "already multi-brand" state the
      known limitation above admits can exist, so on any cluster you are not certain is
      single-issuer today, check first:

        SELECT domain, count(*) FROM wiki_page   GROUP BY domain;
        SELECT domain, count(*) FROM wiki_access GROUP BY domain;

      Exactly one row, reading `default`, is the safe state.

      ── ARMING ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Supply the Liquibase changelog parameter `wiki.restamp.target.domain`, e.g.

          spring.liquibase.parameters.wiki.restamp.target.domain=brand-a

      matching the brand half of the `APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS` pair you are about to set.
      The value must satisfy `DomainRegistry.sanitize()`'s output alphabet
      (`^[a-z0-9-]{1,64}$`) — the registry lower-cases and replaces every other
      character, so "Brand A" configured there becomes "brand-a", and re-stamping to
      "Brand A" here would strand the rows in a domain no principal can ever resolve.
      A value that is absent, blank, "default", or outside that alphabet leaves the
      changeset UNRUN and PENDING (onFail="CONTINUE", not MARK_RAN — a cut-over happens
      later than the first boot, so the changeset must survive every un-armed run and
      still be available when it is finally armed).

      On the ordering of that check, stated plainly: Liquibase changelog parameters are
      TEXTUAL substitution and `sqlCheck` has no bind-parameter form, so the operator's
      raw value is spliced into the very regex check meant to sanitise it. The chain
      still fails CLOSED, and that is measured, not assumed
      (`DefaultDomainRestampChangesetTest` → `ArmingParameterFailsClosed`): an
      injection-shaped value makes the precondition SQL itself unparseable, `onError`
      ="CONTINUE" swallows it, and because the precondition did not PASS the `<sql>`
      block — the only place the value reaches an UPDATE — never runs at all. The
      operator supplying this parameter already owns the database, so this is an
      ordering wart, not a privilege boundary; it is documented so nobody later mistakes
      the regex for a sanitiser applied before substitution.

      ORDER OF OPERATIONS at cut-over: run this migration FIRST, then set
      `APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS`. Between the two the brand's rows are unreachable, so do it
      in one maintenance window.

      ── ROLLBACK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      The inverse re-stamp, `<brand>` -> 'default', over the same four tables. It is
      EXACT only while no row has been written to `<brand>` since the migration ran —
      i.e. roll back BEFORE setting `APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS`, or accept that
      post-cut-over writes are dragged back to 'default' too. The SAME
      `wiki.restamp.target.domain` parameter must be supplied to the rollback run — the
      rollback statements are re-parsed from this changelog, so without it they would
      predicate on the literal, un-substituted placeholder and match nothing.

      PREFER `liquibase rollback-count 1`. Immediately after the armed run the re-stamp
      is the last changeset executed, so `rollback-count 1` undoes exactly it and can
      touch nothing else. That is the primary documented undo.

      `rollback-to-tag pre-default-domain-restamp` also works, but understand its blast
      radius: rollback-to-tag reverses EVERYTHING recorded after the tag, in reverse
      order. That is safe here only because of how the tag is timed — see the tagging
      changeSet below — and only while nothing has been deployed since the cut-over. If
      changesets 008+ have landed and run AFTER the cut-over, rollback-to-tag would take
      them with it; use rollback-count in that case.

      If a rollback would violate `uq_wiki_page_domain_slug` or `uq_wiki_access_active`
      (because a 'default' row sharing the key was written after the migration), the
      statement FAILS and the transaction is rolled back. That is deliberate: a loud
      failure beats a silent merge of two brands' rows. (The forward run has the same
      hazard for the mirror-image reason — see the collision section above.)
      ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    -->

    <!--
      THE NAMED ROLLBACK POINT — and note WHEN it is laid down.

      This tagging changeSet carries the SAME arming precondition as the re-stamp below,
      so it runs in the ARMED cut-over run, immediately before the re-stamp, and not one
      deployment earlier. That timing is the whole point:

        * An UNGATED tag would execute once, at the FIRST deployment of this changelog —
          potentially months before the cut-over. Every changeset 008+ that landed in
          between would then be recorded AFTER the tag, and the `rollback-to-tag` command
          the README hands the operator would reverse those schema changes as well as the
          re-stamp. Gated, the tag is written during the cut-over run itself, so
          everything that landed before the cut-over sits BEFORE it and is out of reach.

        * `runAlways="true"` (Liquibase's usual advice for tagDatabase) is WRONG here and
          was rejected after measuring it: runAlways re-executes the tag on every
          deployment and re-writes its ORDEREXECUTED to the current position, so the
          first deployment AFTER the cut-over would silently slide the tag PAST the
          re-stamp and turn `rollback-to-tag` into a no-op for the one changeset it
          exists to undo. `TagIsLaidDownAtTheCutOverRun` asserts the opposite property —
          the tag still precedes the re-stamp after a further deployment — and that
          assertion fails if anyone adds runAlways later.

      Consequence, stated so it is not a surprise: on an un-armed deployment NO tag is
      laid down. That is correct — before the cut-over there is nothing to roll back to,
      and a tag that names a migration which has not run is a trap, not a safety net.
    -->
    <!-- (No <comment> here: the dbchangelog XSD makes <tagDatabase> an EXCLUSIVE choice
         against every other changeSet child EXCEPT validCheckSum and preConditions, which
         sit in the sequence before it — so a tagging changeSet may be gated, but must
         contain the tag and no <comment>.) -->
    <changeSet id="007-tag-before-default-domain-restamp" author="hugmesh">
        <preConditions onFail="CONTINUE" onError="CONTINUE"
                       onFailMessage="wiki-bff 007: multi-brand cut-over NOT armed, so the pre-re-stamp rollback tag is deliberately NOT laid down. It is written during the armed cut-over run, immediately before the re-stamp, so that rollback-to-tag cannot reach changesets that landed before the cut-over."
                       onErrorMessage="wiki-bff 007: could not evaluate the arming precondition for the rollback tag. Leaving it un-tagged, consistent with the re-stamp being left PENDING.">
            <and>
                <dbms type="postgresql"/>
                <changeLogPropertyDefined property="wiki.restamp.target.domain"/>
                <not>
                    <changeLogPropertyDefined property="wiki.restamp.target.domain" value="default"/>
                </not>
                <sqlCheck expectedResult="1">
                    SELECT CASE WHEN '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}' ~ '^[a-z0-9-]{1,64}$'
                                THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
                </sqlCheck>
            </and>
        </preConditions>
        <tagDatabase tag="pre-default-domain-restamp"/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="007-restamp-default-domain-to-brand" author="hugmesh">
        <!-- INERT UNLESS ARMED. onFail=CONTINUE (NOT MARK_RAN): an un-armed run must
             leave the changeset PENDING so a later cut-over can still run it. onError
             =CONTINUE covers a store where the regex operator below cannot be evaluated
             — the dbms gate is checked first, but an unexpected error must also leave
             the data untouched rather than half-migrated. This precondition is character
             -for-character the one on the tagging changeSet above, so the tag and the
             re-stamp arm and disarm together. -->
        <preConditions onFail="CONTINUE" onError="CONTINUE"
                       onFailMessage="wiki-bff 007: multi-brand cut-over re-stamp NOT armed. Set the Liquibase parameter wiki.restamp.target.domain to the brand from your APP_TENANCY_ISSUERS pair (it must match ^[a-z0-9-]{1,64}$ and must not be 'default'). The changeset stays PENDING until then."
                       onErrorMessage="wiki-bff 007: could not evaluate the arming precondition. Leaving the multi-brand cut-over re-stamp PENDING and the data untouched.">
            <and>
                <!-- Production and the slice-test store are both Postgres; the regex
                     check below is Postgres syntax. Checked first so a non-Postgres
                     store fails the AND before the sqlCheck is reached. -->
                <dbms type="postgresql"/>
                <changeLogPropertyDefined property="wiki.restamp.target.domain"/>
                <not>
                    <changeLogPropertyDefined property="wiki.restamp.target.domain" value="default"/>
                </not>
                <!-- The target must be spellable by DomainRegistry.sanitize(): it
                     lower-cases and replaces every character outside [a-z0-9-], so a
                     value this rejects is a value no JWT issuer can ever resolve to,
                     and re-stamping to it would strand the rows permanently. -->
                <sqlCheck expectedResult="1">
                    SELECT CASE WHEN '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}' ~ '^[a-z0-9-]{1,64}$'
                                THEN 1 ELSE 0 END
                </sqlCheck>
            </and>
        </preConditions>

        <comment>Multi-brand cut-over: re-stamp every domain='default' row in wiki_page,
                 wiki_attachment, wiki_audit_log and wiki_access to the operator-supplied
                 brand. One transaction — all four tables move together or none do.</comment>

        <!-- The four statements are independent (each predicates on its OWN table's
             domain column, none on a join), so the order below is for readability only.
             They run in ONE transaction (Liquibase's default runInTransaction=true), so
             the ACL overlay can never be left behind by a partial failure — and so a
             unique-constraint collision on any one of them leaves ALL four untouched. -->
        <sql>
            UPDATE wiki_page      SET domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}' WHERE domain = 'default';
            UPDATE wiki_attachment SET domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}' WHERE domain = 'default';
            UPDATE wiki_audit_log SET domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}' WHERE domain = 'default';
            UPDATE wiki_access    SET domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}' WHERE domain = 'default';
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>
                UPDATE wiki_access    SET domain = 'default' WHERE domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}';
                UPDATE wiki_audit_log SET domain = 'default' WHERE domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}';
                UPDATE wiki_attachment SET domain = 'default' WHERE domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}';
                UPDATE wiki_page      SET domain = 'default' WHERE domain = '${wiki.restamp.target.domain}';
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
